data.content:
## Migration: EventProcessor to .NET 8

### Problems in the Original Code

| API | Issue on .NET 8 |
|-----|-----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable on `WaitOne` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware |
| Race condition | Multiple enqueues with one `Reset()` can lose signals |

### Patterns Identified

1. **Cancellable work loop** — `while(true)` → check `CancellationToken`
2. **Blocking call interruption** — `WaitOne()` → `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle`
3. **Thread.Abort as forced stop** → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly object _lock = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Wait for signal OR cancellation
            WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _newEvent.WaitHandle, token.WaitHandle });

            if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();              // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);        // wait up to 5s for graceful exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

---

### Key Changes

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `ManualResetEvent` | `ManualResetEventSlim` | Lighter, disposable, exposes `.WaitHandle` on demand |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny(event, token)` | Wakes on cancellation too |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative; signals the WaitHandle |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Loop exits via `IsCancellationRequested` | No forced termination needed |
| Dequeue without count check | `if (_queue.Count > 0)` | Fixes race when multiple wakes drain queue |

---

### Pitfalls to Watch

1. **`WaitOne` doesn't check tokens** — You must use `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle`, or the thread will never wake on cancel.
2. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — It leaks timer handles if not disposed.
3. **Race between `Set`/`Reset`** — If two items enqueue before `Reset()`, you process one and lose the signal. The `Count > 0` check handles this; consider looping to drain.
4. **`Join` without fallback** — If `Handle()` is long-running and doesn't check the token, `Join(5000)` can time out. Pass the token into `Handle` if it does blocking work.

---

### Even Simpler: Use `Channel<T>` (recommended for .NET 8)

If you can go fully modern, `System.Threading.Channels` eliminates all manual synchronization:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _worker;

    public void Start()
    {
        _worker = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();
        _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
        if (_worker is not null)
            await _worker;
    }

    public void Dispose() => _cts.Dispose();

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

This version has no locks, no manual events, no threads, and is fully cancellation-aware out of the box.
